1. Types of exec() functions
The main differences are whether arguments are passed as a list or an array, and whether the executable path is searched using PATH.
Function	Arguments	Searches PATH?
execl()	List of arguments	No
execv()	Array (argv)	No
execlp()	List of arguments	Yes
execvp()	Array (argv)	Yes
execve()	Array (argv) + explicit environment	No
execle()	List of arguments + explicit environment	No


example : 
#include <unistd.h>
#include <cstdio>

int main()
{
    char* args[] = {
        const_cast<char*>("ls"),
        const_cast<char*>("-l"),
        nullptr
    };

    execv("/bin/ls", args);

    // Executes only if execv fails
    perror("execv failed");
    return 1;
}



int main()
{
    char* args[] = {
        const_cast<char*>("printenv"),
        const_cast<char*>("MY_APP"),
        nullptr
    };

    char* env[] = {
        const_cast<char*>("MY_APP=HelloLinux"),
        nullptr
    };

    execve("/usr/bin/printenv", args, env);

    // Executes only if execve fails
    perror("execve failed");
    return 1;
}


Senior-level interview answer: execv() inherits the calling process's environment, while execve() allows the caller to specify the environment explicitly. 
Both replace the current process image, preserve the PID on success, and do not return unless an error occur 
